# game: cassowary, detection and forensics for claude code
#
# name is the project: it is the block in the builder's own config that says
# where this tree's two remotes are. nothing here holds a remote, and a
# builder who has no block for it builds locally.
name cassowary-dist

# the lint cassowary holds. a repository that describes any lint describes
# all of it, so this list is the whole of it.
lint width 80
lint comments
lint exclaim
lint shout
lint paragraph 4
lint words hyperscaler enterprise-grade blazing-fast load-bearing

# the design doc and the readme keep their shape. both are the record of
# what this tool was built against: the design argues from real findings at
# the length the argument takes, and the readme's examples are pasted
# evidence, which wraps where the evidence wrapped. reflowing either to suit
# a width rule edits the exhibit. battle-born, and it shows; that is the
# point of it.
lint allow DESIGN.md paragraph width
lint allow README.md paragraph width shout

# the readme is not a screen and is not meant to be. it opens with a
# cassowary drawn out of the phrases this tool exists to detect, and the rest
# is worked examples of real findings. cutting it to twenty-five lines would
# remove the evidence, which in a forensics tool is the product. the card
# rule is waived for this one page by name rather than by the dotfile's
# absence. its section headings are capitals because that is what a manpage
# calls them, which is why shout is allowed on it too.
lint allow README.md rows

# two tools stay on the road and never enter a release. they measure the
# operator's own working hours, tuned from her rows, and that is hers and
# lives on this machine.
#
# omit, not exclude: exclude scopes what the sweep reads, and a file nobody
# reads still ships. omit keeps it out of the released tree.
omit tools/hours.py
omit tools/load-profile.py

# the suite, as a target, so a stack build runs it the way a person does.
# style-check is a hook, so its test drives it as a subprocess with one
# payload per case and checks the negatives as hard as the positives.
target test . :: python3 tools/style-check-test.py

# THE SCARS, DECLARED.
#
# this is a forensics tool and its subject was this estate: the marks are
# the product, not a leak. a reader who wants to know where a finding came
# from can see it. each is named by file and by kind so that a real leak in
# the same file is still found, which excluding the file or overriding the
# sweep would both hide.
#
# the two tools that measured the operator's own hours are not here. they
# are excluded above, and they stay on the road.
sweep allow DESIGN.md paths
sweep allow NOTICE author
sweep allow README.md sessions word author
sweep allow tools/agents-by-dir.py paths word author
sweep allow tools/commit-claims.py paths
sweep allow tools/daylog.py paths
sweep allow tools/export-extract.py sessions
sweep allow tools/fleetlog.py paths word
sweep allow tools/freeze-audit.py paths word author
sweep allow tools/image-extract.py paths
sweep allow tools/session-extract.py paths
sweep allow tools/style-check-test.py sessions emails
sweep allow tools/style-check.py paths author
